  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 AM EDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nEarly morning water vapor imagery shows a well-defined trough moving \nacross the Canadian Prairie, and another midlevel low spinning over \nthe Rockies. To the east, the midlevel ridge axis is directed right \nover the Great Lakes. At the surface, this all translates to surface \nlows currently centered over Manitoba and Oklahoma, and high pressure \ncentered over central Ontario. Closer to home, we remain under a \nblanket of low clouds, though some mid and upper-level cloud cover \nassociated with Plains convection is beginning to stream into the \narea amid southerly flow developing aloft. Ample cloud cover has \nkept temperatures several degrees warmer than expected tonight, with \nmost of the area currently in the mid 40s and only expected to \nbottom out in the lower 40s and perhaps upper 30s. \n\nGuidance continues to agree on the Canadian and Rockies features to \nphase near NE/SD this afternoon, with a surface low deepening to \n1007-1009mb near Chicago by 00Z Friday. Warm advection out ahead of \nthis kicks off a batch of widespread rain across the area this \nafternoon and evening, with this initial batch dropping widespread \ntotals around 0.50-0.75\" by 06Z. The cold front passes through the \nsecond half of the night, with 850mb temperatures eventually falling \nto around -5 to -7C into Friday afternoon. Thus, expect a transition \nover to lake effect/enhanced rain from 06Z onwards, with rain \ngradually becoming isolated to the northerly wind belts Friday \nmorning and afternoon. Additional totals around 0.25-0.75\" are \nexpected, with the highest across the upsloping areas over the north-\ncentral UP and the Porcupines. Wouldn't surprise me to see some wet \nsnow mix into areas of the interior highlands early and then late \nFriday as freezing levels fall to ~1000 ft AGL, but no accumulations \nare expected. \n\nMeanwhile, Friday morning through Fri afternoon the surface low \nfeature deepens while departing northeast atop the Lower Lakes, \nplacing Upper Michigan on the northwestern flank. Strong cold air \nadvection and stout pressure rises (upwards of 1-2mb/hr) will \ncontribute to northerly wind speeds reaching 25-35 kts gusting 45-50 \nkts on Lake Superior and the adjacent lakeshores. There is still \nsome uncertainty regarding the maximum gust potential as ensembles \n(NBM and ENS) have decreased the probability of Storm Force gusts in \nthe central lake (20-50% chance) and model soundings don't highlight \nmixing into any 50 kts gust layers. \n\nAs winds settle late Friday and dry high pressure clears skies from \nwest to east, temperatures will drop like a rock overnight into \nSaturday morning, reaching the mid to low 20s for much if not all of \nthe interior while some lakeshores may reach freezing for the first \ntime this fall. Ridging and dry weather dominate through the rest of \nthe weekend with the potential for another warm up / fire weather \npattern Sunday into Monday. Similar to last weekend's fire weather \nsetup, ensembles suggest a 50-75% chance for S to SW winds gusting \nto 35 mph in the typical downslope locations of the west and central \nUP and minimum RHs falling to near 30% in the interior west and \ncentral. Luckily, temperatures are only slated to peak into the 60s. \nFire partners and adjacent parties should continue to monitor the \nforecast as containment and mop up efforts for ongoing wildfires may \nstill be ongoing.",
